  "verbalization": "Dextromethorphan, a common cough suppressant, is linked to Rhinitis through a chain of indications that involve respiratory conditions. The evidence suggests that Dextromethorphan may be associated with Rhinitis via the Common Cold or Nasopharyngitis, both of which are linked to Guaifenesin or Clofedanol, respectively. These intermediaries provide a pathway through which Dextromethorphan could influence nasal inflammation. The overlapping indications for Rhinitis across different pathways highlight a potential role for Dextromethorphan in modulating respiratory inflammation, though the exact mechanism remains unclear. The strength of the evidence is supported by multiple independent pathways, suggesting a plausible but not yet fully elucidated relationship between Dextromethorphan and Rhinitis.",
